{
  "id": "80835",
  "type": "Page",
  "tags": [],
  "title": "The Ionization of Hydrated Metal Ions",
  "content": {
    "bibrefs": [],
    "model": [
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "If we measure the pH of the solutions of a variety of metal ions, we will find that these ions act as weak acids when in solution. The aluminum ion is an example. When aluminum nitrate dissolves in water, the aluminum ion reacts with water to give a hydrated aluminum ion, Al(H"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "O)"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "6"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "3+"
              },
              {
                "text": ", dissolved in bulk water. This means that the aluminum ion has the strongest interactions with the six closest water molecules (the so-called first "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "solvation shell"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "The layer of solvent molecules that are closest to, and directly interact with, a dissolved ion or molecule."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "612089052",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "1149900637",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": "), even though it does interact with the other water molecules surrounding this Al(H"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "O)"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "6"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "3+"
              },
              {
                "text": " cluster as well:"
              }
            ],
            "id": "4y4m0jfy0qji3zr",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "pus2rbg0pm47nvj",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "ozk6g9tjb7602gi",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "\\mathrm{Al}({\\mathrm{NO}}_3)_3(s)\\;+\\;6\\;{\\mathrm H}_2\\mathrm O(l)\\;\\;\\longrightarrow\\;\\;\\mathrm{Al}({\\mathrm H}_2\\mathrm O)_6^{3+}(aq)\\;+\\;3\\;{\\mathrm{NO}}_3^-(aq)",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "We frequently see the formula of this ion written simply as “Al"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "3+"
              },
              {
                "text": "("
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "aq"
              },
              {
                "text": ")” without explicitly noting that six water molecules are covalently bonded to the aluminum ion. This is similar to the simplification of the formula of the hydronium ion, H"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "text": "O"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "+"
              },
              {
                "text": " to H"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "+"
              },
              {
                "text": ". In this case, a bonded water molecule acts as a weak acid and donates a proton to a water molecule."
              }
            ],
            "id": "zy94i64gbelr82j",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "ne9tgdtixorydrl",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "hfhb4x98eg7wuby",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "\\mathrm{Al}({\\mathrm H}_2\\mathrm O)_6^{3+}(aq)\\;+\\;{\\mathrm H}_2\\mathrm O(l)\\;\\;\\rightleftharpoons\\;\\; {\\mathrm H}_3\\mathrm O^+(aq)\\;+\\;\\mathrm{Al}({\\mathrm H}_2\\mathrm O)_5(\\mathrm{OH})^{2+}(aq)\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\; K_{\\mathrm a}\\;=\\;1.4\\times10^{-5}",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "The conjugate base produced by this process contains five other bonded water molecules that can act as acids, and so the sequential or stepwise transfer of protons is possible, as shown in the equations below: "
              }
            ],
            "id": "kyko53h49mnq2ev",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "3a7kk7qobcws7n1",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "lzbto3es6j8v4et",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "\\begin{array}{l}\\mathrm{Al}({\\mathrm H}_2\\mathrm O)_6^{3+}(aq)\\;+\\;{\\mathrm H}_2\\mathrm O(l)\\;\\;\\rightleftharpoons\\;\\;{\\mathrm H}_3\\mathrm O^+(aq)\\;+\\;\\mathrm{Al}({\\mathrm H}_2\\mathrm O)_5(\\mathrm{OH})^{2+}(aq)\\;\\;\\;\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{Al}({\\mathrm H}_2\\mathrm O)_5(\\mathrm{OH})^{2+}(aq)\\;+\\;{\\mathrm H}_2\\mathrm O(l)\\;\\;\\rightleftharpoons\\;\\;{\\mathrm H}_3\\mathrm O^+(aq)\\;+\\;\\mathrm{Al}({\\mathrm H}_2\\mathrm O)_4(\\mathrm{OH})_2^+(aq)\\;\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{Al}({\\mathrm H}_2\\mathrm O)_4(\\mathrm{OH})_2^+(aq)\\;+\\;{\\mathrm H}_2\\mathrm O(l)\\;\\;\\rightleftharpoons\\;\\;{\\mathrm H}_3\\mathrm O^+(aq)\\;+\\;\\mathrm{Al}({\\mathrm H}_2\\mathrm O)_3(\\mathrm{OH})_3(aq)\\end{array}",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "alt": "A reaction is shown using ball and stick models. On the left, inside brackets with a superscript of 3 plus outside to the right is structure labeled “[ A l ( H subscript 2 O ) subscript 6 ] superscript 3 plus.” Inside the brackets is s central grey atom to which 6 red atoms are bonded in an arrangement that distributes them evenly about the central grey atom. Each red atom has two smaller white atoms attached in a forked or bent arrangement. Outside the brackets to the right is a space-filling model that includes a red central sphere with two smaller white spheres attached in a bent arrangement. Beneath this structure is the label “H subscript 2 O.” A double sided arrow follows. Another set of brackets follows to the right of the arrows which have a superscript of two plus outside to the right. The structure inside the brackets is similar to that on the left, except a white atom is removed from the structure. The label below is also changed to “[ A l ( H subscript 2 O ) subscript 5 O H ] superscript 2 plus.” To the right of this structure and outside the brackets is a space filling model with a central red sphere to which 3 smaller white spheres are attached. This structure is labeled “H subscript 3 O superscript plus.”",
            "caption": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "When an aluminum ion reacts with water, the hydrated aluminum ion becomes a weak acid."
                  }
                ],
                "type": "p"
              }
            ],
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "fjhwgewxiyslsyk",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "0ol30rb835wsrzf",
            "src": "https://d2xvti2irp4c7t.cloudfront.net/media/64/6432e4dfaa70f5beea939cf6ffabe661/CNX_Chem_14_04_hydronium.jpg",
            "type": "img",
            "width": "600"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Additional examples of the first stage in the ionization of hydrated metal ions are:"
              }
            ],
            "id": "qi5khw49ohing6b",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "ebspym7i6p31zul",
